 are more likely to change jobs than before.\n- This option introduces a potential issue with employee turnover, but it does not directly address the cost reduction claim made by the department manager. It could potentially weaken the argument, but it is not as directly related to the cost reduction as option A.\n\nC) Employees who are proficient in using m-type computers will demand higher income than those who are expected to have no experience in using computers.\n- This option suggests that replacing M-type computers with V-computers might lead to higher employee demands for higher salaries, which could affect the overall cost structure. However, it does not directly address the cost reduction claim made by the department manager. It could potentially weaken the argument, but it is not as directly related to the cost reduction as option A.\n\nD) The average productivity of employees in the general manager's company is lower than that of its competitors.\n- This option provides information about the productivity of the company's employees, but it does not directly address the cost reduction claim made by the department manager. It could potentially weaken the argument, but it is not as directly related to the cost reduction as option A. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
