 or fourth speaker, but since J is the fourth speaker, N cannot be the second speaker either. Therefore, L must be the third speaker, and N cannot be the third speaker. So, option B is incorrect.\n\n**Option C: G or L**.\n- If G is the third speaker, then L cannot be the third speaker because G must speak before lunch, and there must be only one speaker between M and N. This means M and N must be either the second and fourth speakers or the first and third speakers, but since J is the fourth speaker, M and N cannot be the second and fourth speakers. Therefore, G must be the third speaker, and L cannot be the third speaker. So, option C is incorrect.\n\n**Option D: M or N**.\n- If M is the third speaker, then N cannot be the third speaker because there must be only one speaker between M and N. This means N must be either the second or fourth speaker, but since J is the fourth speaker, N cannot be the second speaker either. Therefore, M must be the third speaker, and N cannot be the third speaker. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
