ise.\n\nB) People often drink coffee after a meal consisting of several types of foods that themselves rapidly break down into sugars during digestion.\n- This option suggests that coffee may cause blood-glucose levels to rise because it is consumed after a meal containing rapidly digestible sugars. This directly explains the observed effect of coffee on blood-glucose levels.\n\nC) For many people, the consumption of one piece of chocolate cake has the same stimulating effect as one cup of plain coffee.\n- This option compares the effects of coffee and chocolate cake on blood-glucose levels. While it shows that both can cause blood-glucose levels to rise, it does not specifically explain why coffee causes this effect.\n\nD) Drinking more than two cups of coffee in an hour increases a person's level of stress, and the body reacts to stress by releasing stored glucose into the blood.\n- This option suggests that coffee increases stress levels, which in turn causes the body to release stored glucose into the blood. However, it does not directly explain why coffee causes blood-glucose levels to rise. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
